e=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yin-t&#39;i (\u79b5)\">Yin-t'i (\u79b5)</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yunzhi,_Prince_Zhi\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Yunzhi, Prince Zhi\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367947\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367947\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3678174</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 2 \u2014 Yin-t&#39;i (\u7994)</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Fang Chao-ying</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"929\" data-page-number=\"929\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/327\" data-page-index=\"327\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/327\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;327\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>YIN</b>-t'i \u80e4\u7994, Mar. 12, 1672\u20131734, Nov. 25, was the eldest son of Emperor Sh\u00eang-tsu. He often accompanied the Emperor on the latter's tours, and in 1690 was sent to assist his uncle, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Fu-ch%27%C3%BCan\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Fu-ch&#39;\u00fcan\">Fu-ch'\u00fcan</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], in the expedition against <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Galdan\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Galdan\">Galdan</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] in Jehol. He was recalled, however, before the battle of Ulan-butung took place because he had disputed with Fu-ch'\u00fcan. At the time of the expedition against Galdan in Mongolia in 1696 he was sent with <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Songgotu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Songgotu\">Songgotu</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] to command the advance guard awaiting the Emperor at Torin, and after the Emperor returned to Peking he remained behind to make awards to the victorious troops. Made in 1698 a prince of the second degree with the designation Chih \u76f4, he began to live outside the Palace in his own establishment.\n</p><p>Before long there ensued among the princes a struggle for the throne. <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yin-j%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yin-j\u00eang\">Yin-j\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], the Heir Apparent, was evidently unsuited for the position. Nevertheless it seems that he had the support of a brother, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yin-chih\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yin-chih\">Yin-chih</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], whereas Yin-t'i and several other princes took up the case of another brother, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yin-ss%C5%AD\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yin-ss\u016d\">Yin-ss\u016d</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. In 1708 when Yin-j\u00eang, after a spell of insanity, was degraded as he was returning from Jehol, he was placed in the custody of Yin-t'i. While exercising this responsibility Yin-t'i reminded the Emperor that physiognomists had predicted Yin-ss\u016d's succession to the throne and that it would be easy to get rid of Yin-j\u00eang without leaving any imputation of blame upon the Emperor himself. For this malevolent suggestion Yin-t'i was severely reprimanded. Late in the same year (1708) he was accused by Yin-chih of employing a Lama sorceress to cast a\n&#32;<span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"930\" data-page-number=\"930\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 2.pdf/328\" data-page-index=\"328\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;2.pdf/328\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;328\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>spell on Yin-j\u00eang. Investigation seemed to substantiate the accusation when certain objects believed to have caused Yin-j\u00eang's insanity were dug up in Yin-t'i's courtyard. On this and other counts Yin-t'i was pleced under surveillance, and after being deprived of his titles, was imprisoned in his own courtyard where he died in 1734 and was buried with the rites accorded to a prince of the fourth degree. Most of his property and the Bannermen assigned as his slaves were given to the Emperor's fourteenth son, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yin-t%27i_(%E7%A6%B5)\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yin-t&#39;i (\u79b5)\">Yin-t'i</a></span> [\u79b5, <i>q.v.</i>].\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/226/2a; <i>Tung-hua lu</i>, K'ang-hsi 47:9, 10, 11; <i>Ch'ing Huang-shih ss\u016d-p'u</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Fu-lung-an\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Fu-lung-an\">Fu-lung-an</a></span>) 3/12a.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Fang Chao-ying</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"error\"></span>\n</p></div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Fu-ch'\u00fcan"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Fu-lung-an"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Galdan"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Songgotu"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yin-chih"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yin-j\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yin-ss\u016d"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yin-t'ang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yin-t'i (\u79b5)"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Fang Chao-ying"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 2\n | onlysection = yin-t'i (\u7994)\n | previous = Yin-t'ang\n | next     = Yin-t'i (\u79b5)\n | from     = 327\n | to       = 328\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Fang Chao-ying\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Yin-t'i}}"}}}